# ==========================================================================
#  Q135  The XPath maths library
# ==========================================================================
#
#  ASKS
#    Compute a distance with math:sqrt and a growth figure with math:pow.
#
#  HOW IT WORKS
#    math: is the XPath 3.1 maths library. Jena registers it, and so does
#    HOLOS from 0.31.0, and with it the editor. It covers what afn: covers
#    and rather more -- pow, log, exp, the trigonometric functions -- and
#    unlike afn: it is at least a published specification, even if not one
#    SPARQL requires.
#
#  DIAGRAM
#          math:sqrt(x)        math:pow(x, y)      math:exp(x)
#          math:log(x)         math:log10(x)       math:sin  cos  tan
#          math:atan2(y, x)    math:pi()
#
#        measured on the three engines:
#
#          math:sqrt   editor  yes     holos  yes     fuseki  yes
#          math:pow    editor  yes     holos  yes     fuseki  yes
#
#        It used to be the narrowest of the extension libraries in
#        this course: Fuseki alone, until HOLOS took it on in 0.31.0
#        (SemOps System's function library), and the editor with it.
#        Now all three answer, and value for value. afn:sqrt (q101)
#        covers the square root on all three too; math: has the
#        better claim, because it is a published specification.
#
#        +----------------------------------------------------------+
#        |  What to take from module 15 as a whole: there are four   |
#        |  function libraries here and no two engines agree on      |
#        |  which they have. Write the standard version (q104),      |
#        |  measure whether it is fast enough, and only then reach   |
#        |  for a library -- with a comment saying which engine you  |
#        |  have just tied yourself to.                              |
#        +----------------------------------------------------------+
#
#
#  WHAT TO TAKE AWAY
#    - math: is the XPath maths library: sqrt, pow, log, exp and the
#      trigonometric functions. Jena has it, and HOLOS and the editor
#      have it from 0.31.0.
#    - It overlaps afn: without replacing it. Both run on all three
#      engines here; an engine you have not measured may have neither.
#    - Four libraries, three engines, no agreement. That is the state of
#      extension functions, and it is why the portable rewrite matters.
#
#  NOTE
#    All three engines from HOLOS 0.31.0, which is the editor's engine too;
#    before that HOLOS and the editor raised on math:, and only Fuseki
#    answered. The portable version of this is q104.
#
#  DATA     bookshop-trail-full.ttl
#  LOAD IT  https://semantechs.co.uk/turtle-editor-viewer/?dot=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fpwin%2FSPARQL_Course%2Fmain%2Fdata%2Fbookshop-trail-full.ttl
#  RUNS ON  editor, holos, fuseki
#  SPEC     SPARQL 1.2 Query 17.3.1 Operator Extensibility
#           https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql12-query/#x17-3-1-operator-extensibility
#           SPARQL 1.2 Query 17.6 Extensible Value Testing
#           https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql12-query/#x17-6-extensible-value-testing
#  RETURNS  8 rows, on every engine that runs it
# ==========================================================================

PREFIX bt:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/>
PREFIX bs:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#>
PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>
PREFIX math: <http://www.w3.org/2005/xpath-functions/math#>

SELECT ?name ?km ?doubled
WHERE {
  bt:place-york bs:easting ?ye ; bs:northing ?yn .
  ?shop a           bs:Bookshop ;
        rdfs:label  ?name ;
        bs:easting  ?se ;
        bs:northing ?sn .
  BIND( (?se - ?ye) * (?se - ?ye) + (?sn - ?yn) * (?sn - ?yn) AS ?d2 )
  BIND( ROUND( math:sqrt(?d2) / 100 ) / 10 AS ?km )
  BIND( ROUND( math:pow(?km, 2) )          AS ?doubled )
}
ORDER BY ?km
LIMIT 8
